$article=['date'=>'2027-01-28','slug'=>'social-media-teen-mental-health','tag'=>'Teen Mental Health','category'=>'Teen Mental Health','breadcrumb'=>'Social Media and Teen Mental Health','meta_title'=>'Social Media and Teen Mental Health: What Evidence Shows','description'=>'Understand what social media can and cannot explain about teen mental health, which patterns raise concern and how families can set collaborative boundaries.','h1'=>'What Social Media Is Actually Doing to Your Teenager&rsquo;s Mental Health','dek'=>'The useful question is not whether social media is simply good or bad, but what this teenager is doing, experiencing and losing because of it.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Social media can support friendship, identity and community while also exposing teenagers to comparison, bullying, disrupted sleep and compulsive use. Research does not justify blaming every mental health difficulty on a phone. Effects vary by person, platform, content and context.</p><h2>Look at the pattern of use</h2><p>Active conversation with supportive friends differs from hours of passive comparison or hostile content. Ask how your teen feels before, during and after use, and whether they can stop when they intend to.</p><h2>Watch what is being displaced</h2><p>Concern rises when use repeatedly replaces sleep, meals, movement, schoolwork or in-person relationships. Night-time notifications and scrolling can be practical targets even when the platform is not the original cause of distress.</p><h2>Social anxiety can move online</h2><p>Posting, checking reactions and fear of exclusion can amplify self-consciousness. For another teen, online communication may provide a manageable bridge to connection. Avoid assuming the same effect for everyone.</p><h2>Create boundaries collaboratively</h2><p>Discuss device-free sleep time, privacy, harassment, content curation and what to do after seeing something disturbing. Adults should model the boundaries they request. Sudden confiscation without understanding context may remove a genuine support network.</p><h2>When to seek help</h2><p>Persistent withdrawal, panic, severe sleep change, school refusal, self-harm or major functional decline deserves professional attention whether or not social media is involved. Explore <a href="/teen-mental-health/social-anxiety-in-teens">support for social anxiety in teens</a>.</p>
HTML,'faqs'=>[['Does social media cause depression in teenagers?','The relationship is complex and not identical for every teenager. Content, comparison, bullying, sleep, existing vulnerability and type of use all affect the pattern.'],['How much screen time is safe for a teen?','No single number captures content, purpose and displacement. Families should protect sleep, school, movement, relationships and safety while agreeing on realistic, reviewable boundaries.'],['Should I take my teenager&rsquo;s phone away?','Immediate restriction may be needed for specific safety risks. Otherwise, understand the problem first and create collaborative boundaries that do not unexpectedly remove supportive relationships.'],['When does social media use need professional help?','Seek help when use is compulsive, connected with severe distress or bullying, or contributes to persistent problems with sleep, school, relationships, safety or daily functioning.']],
